1. What problem does Prototype solve?
      Imagine you're building an insurance platform. 
      A customer requests a new policy based on an existing policy template containing dozens of configuration values.
      
      You could create a new object and manually copy every field. 
      But this approach creates repetitive code and risks missing fields whenever the model evolves.
      
      The Prototype Pattern creates a new object by copying an existing object, 
      called the prototype, instead of constructing the new object entirely from scratch.


==================================================================================================================================================================
The key idea is copy an existing object's state rather than repeat its construction logic.

=====================================================================================================================================================
2. Implement Prototype in Java 21:

Let's use an insurance policy template as our example.
Step 1 — Create the prototype class

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

public final class InsurancePolicyTemplate {

    private final String policyType;
    private final BigDecimal basePremium;
    private final List<String> addOnCoverCodes;

    public InsurancePolicyTemplate(
            String policyType,
            BigDecimal basePremium,
            List<String> addOnCoverCodes) {

        this.policyType = Objects.requireNonNull(policyType);
        this.basePremium = Objects.requireNonNull(basePremium);
        this.addOnCoverCodes = new ArrayList<>(
                Objects.requireNonNull(addOnCoverCodes));
    }

    // Copy constructor
    public InsurancePolicyTemplate(
            InsurancePolicyTemplate source) {

        this(
                source.policyType,
                source.basePremium,
                source.addOnCoverCodes
        );
    }

    public InsurancePolicyTemplate copy() {
        return new InsurancePolicyTemplate(this);
    }

    public String getPolicyType() {
        return policyType;
    }

    public BigDecimal getBasePremium() {
        return basePremium;
    }

    public List<String> getAddOnCoverCodes() {
        return List.copyOf(addOnCoverCodes);
    }
}

=====================================================================================================================================================
Notice three important design decisions:
      - The copy constructor reuses the existing object's configuration.
      - The list is copied, so the new object doesn't share the same mutable list.
      - The class is final, and its fields are final, preventing subclasses from 
          changing the copying behavior and preventing reassignment of those fields.

The copy method creates a new object; it does not return the original instance



