Step 2 — Create and copy the prototype

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class PrototypeDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Create the prototype once
        InsurancePolicyTemplate motorTemplate =
                new InsurancePolicyTemplate(
                        "MOTOR",
                        new BigDecimal("12000.00"),
                        List.of("ZERO_DEP", "ROADSIDE_ASSISTANCE")
                );

        // Create two independent copies
        InsurancePolicyTemplate customerA =
                motorTemplate.copy();

        InsurancePolicyTemplate customerB =
                motorTemplate.copy();

        System.out.println(customerA.getPolicyType());
        System.out.println(customerB.getBasePremium());

        System.out.println(customerA != motorTemplate); // true
        System.out.println(customerB != motorTemplate); // true
        System.out.println(customerA != customerB);     // true
    }
}

Both copies begin with the same configuration, but each is a separate object.

In this example, the class is immutable from the caller's perspective, 
so the copies can safely share immutable values such as String and BigDecimal. 
The add-on list is independently copied.

Production consideration: 
          This example demonstrates copying configuration. 
          A real customer policy would normally need its own customer ID, policy number, 
          effective dates, and other identity-specific fields. 

          Do not blindly copy identifiers or financial state from an existing customer policy.

